```
# ============================================================================
# Detection: Mass File Modification Indicative of Ransomware Encryption
# Detection ID: DE-IM-001
# MITRE ATT&CK: T1486 - Data Encrypted for Impact
# Data Source: Sysmon EventCode=11 (FileCreate), FSRM/File Server Audit
# Severity: Critical
# ============================================================================

index=endpoint sourcetype="XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" EventCode=11
TargetFilename="*.*"
| rex field=TargetFilename "\\.(?<ext>[a-zA-Z0-9]{2,10})$"
| bucket _time span=2m
| stats dc(TargetFilename) as files_touched values(ext) as extensions by ComputerName, Image, _time
| where files_touched>=50
| sort -files_touched
```

## Detection Logic
Legitimate processes rarely create/rewrite 50+ distinct files within a 2-minute window; ransomware encryption routines do this by nature. Tracking distinct file extensions written in the same burst also surfaces the telltale new-extension-per-file pattern many ransomware families exhibit.

## False Positive Considerations
Backup software, file sync clients (OneDrive/Dropbox bulk sync), and antivirus full-scans with quarantine actions can trigger this — exclude known backup/sync process images by verified hash.

## Tuning Notes
This should be wired to an automated response action (host network isolation via EDR API) given the time-criticality of ransomware — see `/scripts/incident_report_generator.py` and PB-001 for the paired response workflow.
